WEBVTT

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.820
This is a hedge fund trading desk,

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.140
built as a single LangGraph:

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.420
nineteen nodes, with six analysts,

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:11.700
a bull and a bear,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.200
four strategy desks,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.760
a risk committee, and an execution router.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.080
Everything you are about to see is a real

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.220
run, recorded against the live graph

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.420
with real models.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.120
Nodes never call each other.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.100
They share one typed state,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.280
thirteen channels.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.120
Each node reads what it needs

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.420
and returns only the keys it changed.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.680
When parallel branches write to the same

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.840
channel, a reducer decides how to combine

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.020
them: five channels here merge dictionaries,

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.960
so six analysts can write at once without

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:43.080
overwriting each other.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.840
The run starts at the CIO router,

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.660
which fans out in two ways.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.720
Six analysts start at once.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.580
And a Send call spawns one deep research

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.640
worker per ticker:

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.020
the same function,

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:57.740
mapped over six inputs.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.100
They all run in one super-step,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.120
so the aggregator fires only

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.720
when the slowest branch lands,

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.360
and the reducers have merged twelve parallel

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:07.560
writes into the state.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.800
The aggregator feeds two nodes

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:11.940
that run in parallel:

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.340
a bull advocate and a bear critic,

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:15.580
reading the same state

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.440
and arguing opposing cases.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.760
Then the arbiter rules.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.540
In this run it settled on market neutral

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.500
dispersion, and split the capital:

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.080
15% momentum, 35% stat arb,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.700
30% macro vol, 20% event arb.

00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:34.407
Next, the desks.

00:01:34.987 --> 00:01:37.607
The arbiter returns one Send per desk,

00:01:38.027 --> 00:01:39.907
but there is only one desk subgraph,

00:01:40.607 --> 00:01:41.967
compiled once with its own input

00:01:41.987 --> 00:01:43.127
and output schemas,

00:01:43.327 --> 00:01:45.327
and run four times in parallel.

00:01:45.807 --> 00:01:47.287
A quant models the legs,

00:01:47.647 --> 00:01:49.107
a desk head signs off,

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:51.527
and the quant's draft stays in a private channel.

00:01:52.087 --> 00:01:55.007
Only the final proposal flows back to shared state.

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:57.113
Now the gate.

00:01:57.773 --> 00:01:59.073
Two auditors run in parallel,

00:01:59.373 --> 00:02:01.233
and the chief risk officer reads them.

00:02:01.553 --> 00:02:03.253
But the language model does not get the

00:02:03.273 --> 00:02:05.553
final say: a deterministic risk engine

00:02:05.573 --> 00:02:07.673
computes exposure and value at risk,

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:09.433
and its verdict is authoritative.

00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:11.573
Here it flags the momentum desk:

00:02:11.833 --> 00:02:13.873
a TLT position of $14M,

00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:16.293
against a $12M cap.

00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:19.993
The conditional edge returns the verdict

00:02:20.013 --> 00:02:22.453
revise, and the router sends only the

00:02:22.473 --> 00:02:23.913
offending desk around again,

00:02:23.933 --> 00:02:25.213
with a fresh Send.

00:02:25.753 --> 00:02:27.053
The revision counter moves

00:02:27.073 --> 00:02:28.633
from zero to one.

00:02:29.313 --> 00:02:31.913
Momentum comes back with a book of $11.5M

00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:34.193
gross, the engine re-checks,

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:35.933
and this time it approves:

00:02:36.393 --> 00:02:38.473
about 98.5% gross,

00:02:38.873 --> 00:02:42.593
9.5% net, value at risk under the limit.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.860
The loop is bounded to two rounds.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:47.860
If breaches are still open after

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.420
that, the graph does not approve by default:

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.360
a rejected verdict routes to a halt node,

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.520
and the run ends with no trade.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.320
That branch did not fire in this recording;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.020
the test suite covers it.

00:03:00.967 --> 00:03:03.647
Approved, so the head of trading releases

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:07.107
the blotter: $25.2M gross,

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:08.587
scaled and hedged.

00:03:08.867 --> 00:03:11.147
The execution router slices seven parent

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:12.387
orders into child orders

00:03:12.407 --> 00:03:13.627
across dark pools,

00:03:14.207 --> 00:03:15.747
IEX and lit venues,

00:03:16.007 --> 00:03:19.147
with about $43k of estimated cost,

00:03:19.407 --> 00:03:20.867
and the run reaches its end.

00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:24.493
One shared state, parallel fan-outs,

00:03:24.933 --> 00:03:26.793
a subgraph reused four times,

00:03:27.213 --> 00:03:29.173
a deterministic gate with a bounded loop,

00:03:29.513 --> 00:03:30.773
and a fail-closed exit.

00:03:31.493 --> 00:03:32.513
Run it with langgraph dev

00:03:32.533 --> 00:03:33.973
and watch it in Studio.

00:03:34.513 --> 00:03:36.113
It is a demo on paper data,

00:03:36.413 --> 00:03:37.813
and it is not trading advice.

